WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.960
Most people think that if you want to

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.880
make extra money on the side, you either

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.480
have to pick up more hours at work or go

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.839
drive for Door Dash. But there are

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.760
businesses out there that you could

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:12.160
start this week and most of them for

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.920
around $500 and you could run them at

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.440
nights or on weekends without ever

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.880
quitting your job. And a couple of

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.720
people that I'm going to show you today

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.640
took that exact kind of thing and turned

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.480
it into a company doing hundreds of

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.320
thousands of dollars a year. So, my name

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.080
is Austin Zbeck and I've built multiple

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.680
different businesses primarily in real

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.359
estate, pest control, and a couple of

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.039
other industries over the last decade.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:32.960
And if you get any value out of today's

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:34.399
video, all that I ask you to do is just

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.079
smash that like button for the YouTube

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:38.079
algorithm. Subscribe if you haven't

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:39.520
already. It'll help get this video in

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:41.040
front of more people who need to see it.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.719
So, let's start with one where your

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.719
customers are sitting in just about

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:46.399
every parking lot that you pull into,

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:48.160
and that is mobile headlight

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:49.920
restoration. Next time you're out, go

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.600
ahead and just look around at all the

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.520
older cars. You're going to notice that

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.160
the headlights are cloudy and yellow,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.079
and it makes the whole car look old. And

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.840
people will literally pay you to fix

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:01.760
that right in their driveway. John

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.920
Rogers runs a company called Renew

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.960
Headlight Restoration out in western

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.439
North Carolina. And he charges $100 to

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.200
do both headlights. And most cars take

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:12.960
him about an hour and a half. And he

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.040
doesn't make anybody bring their car to

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.040
a shop. He'll go to their house, go to

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.960
their work, or set up somewhere local.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.880
So the customer doesn't lose half their

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.119
day sitting in a waiting room. And that

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.880
convenience is what he's selling. And

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:26.799
think about who needs this the most.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.240
somebody who's about to sell their car.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:29.840
They've already cleaned it out. They're

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.680
taking pictures for the listing and

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.240
those yellow headlights make the front

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.240
just look totally rough. And that person

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.920
has a real reason to care about how it

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.079
turns out. So, if you were to do three

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.880
cars on a Saturday, hypothetically, at

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:44.240
$100 each, that is $300 for the day.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.799
Now, if you were to do that every

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.880
Saturday out of a month, that's $1,200 a

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:51.360
month from 12 customers. And you're only

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.280
working 6 to eight hours on a Saturday

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.200
with drive time included. And the

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.520
startup cost here is next to nothing.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.600
Cerakote sells a hand applied kit for

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.360
about 18 bucks and that'll do two

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.439
headlights. And that kit will come with

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.360
the abrasives, the protective coating.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:07.840
And you don't need any power tools to do

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.759
the job. So between 10 kits, masking

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.360
supplies, some towels, some protective

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.599
gear, and a work light. You're looking

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.360
at about $400 to get started. My

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:17.520
recommendation would be just to practice

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.440
first so you know which lenses you can

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.040
actually fix before anybody pays you

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.239
money. And if you're thinking, well, why

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.160
wouldn't they just buy the kit

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:25.760
themselves? It's the same reason that

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.599
people pay to get their windows cleaned

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.040
or their car cleaned. They want it done

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:30.879
and they don't want to spend their

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.720
Saturday learning how to do it or doing

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:35.280
it themselves. Now, the real money here

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.280
is in the relationships. Small used car

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.519
dealers are getting new inventory all

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.599
the time. So, you could go to them and

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:43.760
offer them a paid trial and then knock

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.360
it out of the park and do multiple cars

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.879
every time you go. And when you're at

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.800
somebody's house, you could look at the

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:50.560
driveway and if they have a second car,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.080
you just obviously did a great job on

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.920
the first car. So you could give them a

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.840
quote on that car. See if they want it

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.280
done at the same time or schedule a time

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.040
to come back and do that one as well.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.120
The next one is the biggest investment

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.599
on today's list, but the guy doing it

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.239
did six figures his very first year, and

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:08.239
that is dryer vent cleaning. Christopher

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:10.400
Fritz got into this business because he

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:12.800
got burned. He hired a cleaner and got

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.879
scammed. Then another operator came out

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.319
and actually fixed the work for him. And

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:18.159
instead of just paying the guy and

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:20.239
moving on, Chris apprenticed with him

00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:21.840
for an entire year. He learned the trade

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:24.159
first and then he started his own

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.640
company, Red's Dryer Vent Cleaning near

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:29.599
Austin, Texas. And he says in his first

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.480
12 months brought him over $100,000 in

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:34.560
gross revenue from more than 700 jobs,

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.239
which is two jobs for every day of the

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.239
year. Okay, now here's why it works.

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:39.760
Homeowners want their appliances taken

00:03:38.239 --> 00:03:41.760
care of, but they don't want to have to

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.760
figure it out themselves. So, you show

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.519
up, you inspect it, you clean it, and

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.760
you show them what you did. And that is

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:49.920
exactly how Chris marketed it. He posted

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.760
videos of his actual job so people could

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.280
see who they were hiring and what the

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.040
appointment looked like before they ever

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.280
picked up the phone. Now, pricing-wise

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.239
here, a provider out in Cape Cod charges

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.000
about $199 for the service. So, if you

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:04.560
were to do three jobs on a Saturday,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
we're looking at about $597.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.760
Now, if you were to do that, let's say

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.480
two Saturdays a month, you're looking at

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.720
that same $1,200. And four Saturdays

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.720
gets you close to $2,400. That's about

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.880
eight hours for three jobs that are

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.720
close together, plus an hour during the

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.639
week for booking. Okay. Now, the caveat

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.400
here is the equipment does cost a little

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:24.880
bit more on this one. A professional

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.120
supplier sells a bundle with the vacuum,

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.240
the rods, the inspection camera, and all

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.840
of the accessories for about $1,950.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.840
And once you add the rest of the tools

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.800
that you need, I would probably plan to

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.160
spend about 2500 to 3,000 to get this up

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.320
and running. And you probably want to

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.080
get trained before your first paid job.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.840
This is one on the list where you really

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.919
need to know what you're doing. But 15

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:50.479
jobs at $199 is $2,985

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:52.160
in sales. That is five Saturdays and

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:54.080
it's about what the equipment costs you.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.840
And the repeat business is built right

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.759
in because you can set up maintenance

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.680
reminders for your customers and go talk

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:01.600
to property managers because one

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.919
property manager could hand you a bunch

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.759
of units in a single day. And if a house

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:07.120
has a second dryer, you could quote it

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:08.639
while you're already standing right

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.400
there. All right, number three is one

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:12.479
that most people would never even think

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.240
of as a business, and that is laundry

00:05:12.479 --> 00:05:16.320
pickup and delivery. And before you

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.840
picture yourself buying laundry mat and

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.520
filling it with a bunch of expensive

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.000
machines, that is not what I'm talking

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.680
about. So Hias's Tucker got started just

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.520
helping out a friend who was completely

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.440
overwhelmed with laundry. She'd go pick

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.280
it up, wash it at her own house in her

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.120
own washer and dryer and bring it back.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.960
That was literally it. Then the moms in

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.120
a local mom's group heard about it and

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.639
they wanted the same thing. So now she's

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.240
picking up clothes from a bunch of

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:40.240
different families and running them all

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.000
through her machines at home. And that

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.160
turned into her company, The Laundry

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.240
Basket. And as it grew, she partnered

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.400
with commercial facilities to do actual

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.680
washing. And in 2025, the company

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.360
reported nearly $470,000

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.039
in revenue. Now, think about who your

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.199
customer actually is here. They own a

00:05:55.039 --> 00:05:58.800
washing machine. That's not the problem.

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:00.720
The problem is is that they get home

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.160
from work, they've got dinner to make,

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.919
there's homework, and there's three

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.600
baskets of laundry sitting there waiting

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:07.600
for them. They don't want a machine.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.759
They want the whole job off their plate.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.680
And here's the best part. Next week, all

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:13.600
those clothes are dirty again. So, this

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.080
is recurring revenue built right into

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.600
the model. Same houses, same day every

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.520
single week. Okay, so let's talk about

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.039
numbers a little bit. One provider out

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.800
in LA charges $29 a pound for pickup and

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:26.960
delivery. Let's just call it two bucks

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.360
for the sake of the conversation. A 25

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.680
lb bag is $50 an order. So if you've got

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.880
10 households on a weekly pickup, you're

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.479
looking at $500 a week or over $2,100 a

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.400
month. Now, if we were to get that up to

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:41.039
30 households, you're averaging about

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.479
$6,500 a month in sales. And if your

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:44.560
partner's doing the washing, let's say,

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:46.400
then 10 households is maybe eight hours

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.160
a week worth of your time. Now, the

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.160
startup cost here is almost nothing.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.919
You're going to need bags, bins, labels,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.280
and a car and a phone that you probably

00:06:51.919 --> 00:06:55.120
already have. The one thing that you

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.199
want to do before your first customer is

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.720
to get a quote from a laundry facility

00:06:57.199 --> 00:07:00.160
if you're going to use that instead of

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.000
your home. Cuz you want to make sure if

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.759
you use a commercial facility that you

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.360
still have margin built in before you

00:07:03.759 --> 00:07:07.520
price your customer. And then there's an

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.319
easy upsell. that same LA provider

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.400
charges $30 to do a king-size comforter.

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:15.360
So, if 10 of your customers add one,

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:17.199
that's an easy extra $300 on pickups

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.720
that you were already picking up. My

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:20.639
suggestion would be to start with one

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.560
neighborhood and one pickup day where

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:23.919
people can count on and then build your

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.840
route from there. All right, moving on

00:07:23.919 --> 00:07:27.360
to the next one. Everything so far has

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.800
been something that people need done.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.280
Number four is something that the

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.440
customer actually wants to show off, and

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:35.840
that is balloon installations and event

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.160
decor. So, Bri Gigglio started a company

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.880
called Bashify while she was working as

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.800
a pediatric ICU nurse. It started with

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.120
pop-up picnics and then it grew to

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.800
events and then into balloons. And

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.720
according to her founder profile, the

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.880
company did $600,000 in revenue in 2025

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.720
across services and e-commerce. Now,

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:56.720
think about who's hiring you here. It's

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.720
a parent planning a birthday party who

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.160
has a picture of exactly what they want.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.160
They don't want to spend their Friday

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.000
night figuring out balloon sizes and

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.000
trying to build a backdrop. They want

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.520
somebody to show up and make the space

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.520
look like the picture. So, the startup

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.440
cost here is extremely low. For a small

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.199
airfield setup, you need a pump,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:15.599
balloons, some sizing tools,

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:17.440
attachments, supports, and bags. And

00:08:15.599 --> 00:08:19.440
some basic equipment could run you about

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.680
$25 bucks. So, with your balloons,

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.599
practice materials, reusable supports,

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.479
tools, and all the extra stuff, you're

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:27.919
all in for probably $5 or $600. Now, if

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:30.080
you were to get really good at a couple

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:31.759
of designs that you can photograph and

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.919
repeat, then you can book your first

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:35.440
party the very next weekend. Now, Bri

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:37.680
has talked about balloon customers

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.800
spending about $500. So, if you were to

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:42.719
do one $500 install every weekend, we're

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.399
looking at $2,000 a month. If you were

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:46.640
to do two a weekend, we're looking at

00:08:44.399 --> 00:08:48.800
$4,000 a month. And each install is

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.640
roughly 3 hours of prep the night before

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.399
and about three more to travel out

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.519
there, get set up, and tear down. And

00:08:52.399 --> 00:08:55.200
then, of course, there's a lot of

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:57.360
upsells you can do here. So, if you were

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.600
to offer, let's say, a backdrop upgrade

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.279
for about an extra $100, then of course

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.040
you can get creative with all the

00:09:01.279 --> 00:09:04.880
additional upgrades or upsells that you

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:06.480
can think of. Now, if all eight of your

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.720
customers that month take it, that's

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.360
another $800 bucks. Okay? So, now you're

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.360
at $4,800 a month just to make sure that

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.040
your extra materials and time are priced

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.680
into it. But the real key in this

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.600
business is relationships, particularly

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.680
event planners. Because a family might

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.440
hire you one single time, but a planner

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.120
could have another event for you the

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.040
very next weekend. So, if you were to

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.200
put together a small portfolio and a

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:31.200
clear menu and make it easy for them to

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.399
picture the setup for their next event,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.080
then they're just going to keep coming

00:09:32.399 --> 00:09:35.839
back to you for more. Okay, the last one

